AB  - This study explores the effect of a GPT-based chatbot integrated within an eclectic writing pedagogy – combining process and genre approaches – on university students’ genre-specific writing skills across narrative, descriptive, process, and argumentative genres. It also examines improvements in key writing aspects: task response, coherence-cohesion, grammatical and structural accuracy, lexical resource, and logical order. Using a sequential mixed-method design with pre- and post-tests, 30 third-year university students participated in 24 sessions involving the chatbot-enhanced pedagogy. Research instruments included pre- and post-writing genre tasks, observation field notes, and semi-structured interviews. Quantitative results showed significant improvements across writing aspects and genres. Task response increased notably in descriptive (M = 2.05 to M = 3.35) and narrative writing (M = 2.10 to M = 3.28). Coherence-cohesion gains were strong in process writing (M = 2.40 to M = 3.65, p < .001). Grammatical accuracy improved in process (M = 1.75 to M = 2.70, p < .001) and argumentative genres (M = 1.60 to M = 2.30, p = .002). Lexical resources fluctuated but recovered by the final descriptive writing task (M = 3.10). Logical order steadily improved in narrative and process writing. Argumentative writing showed modest gains in task response and grammar but a slight decline in coherence-cohesion. Qualitative data revealed positive participant responses and enhanced genre writing skills. However, occasional off-topic chatbot responses and weak genre awareness necessitated student critical evaluation and teacher mediation. Despite promising outcomes, the single-group design, small sample size, and cultural context limit generalizability. This study contributes to chatbot-assisted writing pedagogy, suggesting AI tools can support genre-specific writing when carefully integrated.
