AB  - Civic participation is vital for sustaining democracy, yet systematic disparities in who participates warrant continued attention. Prior research has highlighted the role of education but has paid limited attention to heterogeneity within higher education and its intersection with gender. Using data from the Korea Education and Employment Panel survey, a nationally representative panel of South Korean young adults, we examine how civic outcomes, such as voting, volunteering, and group involvement, vary by educational attainment, from non-college to college completion, and, among college graduates, institutional selectivity and field of study, and how these patterns differ by gender. We further employ causal mediation analysis to identify the pathways linking higher education to civic engagement. The findings indicate that horizontal stratification in higher education does not significantly shape civic participation, whereas less-educated women face pronounced intersectional disadvantages in civic life. Moreover, the civic returns to higher education operate primarily through direct pathways, rather than indirectly through socioeconomic or family-related mediators. These results suggest that expanding access to higher education can foster democratic inclusion, particularly for women who would otherwise be marginalized in civic life.
