AB  - Cross-country differences in education quality have long been studied for their implications at both individual and societal levels, with growing emphasis on the role of quality in realizing the economic returns to education. This study develops a novel cross-country measure of higher education quality by leveraging the relationship between institution-level indicators—such as faculty-to-student ratios and global university rankings—and the earnings of college graduates employed overseas. Building on this relationship, a country-level index of higher education quality is constructed that captures cross-country variation in institutional characteristics, weighted by their estimated effects on graduate earnings, for 98 countries. To assess the macroeconomic relevance of higher education quality, cross-country regressions of gross domestic product per worker, resident patenting activity, and research and development expenditures are estimated. To address potential endogeneity, an instrumental variable strategy is employed that exploits geographic proximity to global academic hubs. The results suggest a substantial and economically meaningful relationship between higher education quality and all three outcomes, highlighting its potential role in shaping long-run economic development and innovation capacity.
