AB  - Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI), exemplified by ChatGPT, is transforming language education by providing sustainable, learner-friendly and formative assessment. However, its impact on second/foreign language (L2) speaking for academic purposes, particularly on strategic competence, remains under-explored. Grounded in Dynamic Systems Theory (DST), this 20-week longitudinal mixed-methods study, a pilot study of a large project with a grant from the National Social Science Fund ofChina, examined how ChatGPT-mediated formative assessment influenced the development of strategic competence and oral proficiency among two Chinese business English majors. Data from strategic competence inventories, research journals, interviews, and oral proficiency assessments were analysed using quantitative modelling and thematic analysis. Findings reveal a nonlinear yet upward trajectory in both constructs, with individual variability in learners' strategic competence development. The four components of strategic competence, operating as four metacognitive strategies (.i.e., planning, problem-solving, monitoring, and self-evaluating) functioned both independently and interactively, with monitoring and self-evaluating showing the strongest links to oral proficiency gains. Moreover, learners reported that ChatGPT provided structured feedback, reduced anxiety, and enhanced self-regulation, though concerns about AI over-reliance emerged. These findings reinforce DST's view of L2 learning as dynamic and adaptive while also contributing to the refinement of the Strategic Competence Model of L2 Speaking (SCMLS) in AI-mediated learning contexts.
